      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. Their problem was that saved searches returned low-relevance hits and duplicates, increasing manual triage time significantly. The proof plan proposed to get them comfortable with renewal is a short-term proof-of-fix involving updating the index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer for the top 20 saved searches. This proof includes a 7-10 business day A/B test and validation aiming for a top-5 correct hit rate of at least 80%, no regression in suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms. \n\nAdditional commercial terms include a 90-day professional services block at a fixed price of $48k, with a 30% discount if BlueHarbor concurrently signs for Orchestrator expansion. There is also a conditional credit: if the proof does not meet acceptance criteria within 10 business days, BlueHarbor will receive a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against their renewal. \n\nCustomer responsibilities are to provide a schema registry export and 14 days of anonymized query logs by 2026-03-19. Northstar will run the A/B test starting 2026-03-22 and deliver a statement of work addendum with the conditional credit clause by 2026-03-19 end of day. Risks include the possibility of needing to reindex approximately 15 TB of data if the mapping is insufficient, which would take about 48 hours of compute and cost around $25k, with an escalation path defined.\n\nThis information is all from the detailed negotiation and retro documents related to BlueHarbor Logistics following the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout [art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].",
